BUILD-113 — does a burst survive, and does the delay decide it? Summary.

Base tested: main at bb44873.
CLI: Claude Code (Ink UI), v2.1.240, unauthenticated ("Not logged in - Please run /login").
Prediction stated before running (architect's, filed in the spec): delay=0 loses
or coalesces markers; delay=0.5 does not, or loses fewer.

Method: pause -> queue N -> resume (the fabric's own burst, no client racing).
  1. office pause sme-2 (confirmed via redis EXISTS ...:paused)
  2. office send x20, unique marker each, while paused (confirmed queued:
     ingress LLEN == 20, pane unchanged, before resuming)
  3. office resume sme-2 (kicks once per queued envelope, drains back to back,
     no client round trip between deliveries)
  4. Waited for ingress to drain to 0, then read the CLI's own session
     transcript for ground truth (not a pane scrape — build 112's finding
     applies more here, not less: Ink's own render only keeps ~5 recent turns).

Two tenants: build113d-0824-1330 (PASTE_ENTER_DELAY unset, default 0.5s),
build113z-0824-1330 (PASTE_ENTER_DELAY=0, baked in via container/.env +
--force-recreate before hiring anyone, confirmed via printenv).

=== RESULT: PREDICTION CONFIRMED ===

  configuration          | sent | opened | unique markers present | true losses | coalescing events
  PASTE_ENTER_DELAY=0.5   |  20  |   20   |          20            |      0      |         1
  PASTE_ENTER_DELAY=0     |  20  |   20   |          16            |      4      |      3 (at least)

⚠ opened-proves-delivery contradiction, observed live: both configurations
read opened 20/20. At delay=0, four envelopes' content NEVER appears anywhere
in the CLI's own transcript — BURSTZ003, BURSTZ004, BURSTZ010, BURSTZ018 —
confirmed absent by `comm -23` against the full 001-020 set. opened says
20 of 20; the CLI received 16.

--- Default (0.5s) detail: one coalescing event, zero true losses ---
Every BURSTD001-020 marker appears at least once in the transcript. But 20
submitted turns carry only 19 distinct message slots' worth of new content:
  turn 1 (11:23:38.772Z): "BURSTD001"                        <- clean
  turn 2 (11:23:39.371Z): "BURSTD001\nBURSTD002"              <- BURSTD001 RESUBMITTED,
                                                                  bundled with BURSTD002
  turns 3-20: one marker each, clean (BURSTD003..BURSTD020)
So BURSTD001 was submitted twice; BURSTD002 never received its own standalone
turn — its only appearance is bundled inside BURSTD001's second submission.
Not a loss (both marker strings are present in the transcript), but not clean
either.

--- Zero (0s) detail: coalescing AND outright loss ---
  turn: "BURSTZ001\nBURSTZ002"                     <- coalesced
  turn: "BURSTZ005\nBURSTZ006"                     <- coalesced
  BURSTZ003, BURSTZ004                             <- MISSING ENTIRELY, no trace
  turn: "BURSTZ011\nBURSTZ012"                      \
  turn: "BURSTZ011\nBURSTZ012\nBURSTZ013"            > both present — 011/012 submitted twice,
                                                       once as a pair, again as part of a triple
  BURSTZ010                                        <- MISSING ENTIRELY, no trace
  BURSTZ018                                        <- MISSING ENTIRELY, no trace
  BURSTZ007, 008, 009, 014, 015, 016, 017, 019, 020 <- each individually clean

Also observed, not investigated further: several markers appear preceded by a
{"type":"queue-operation",...} record before their {"type":"user",...} entry —
an internal Claude Code bookkeeping record, present at both delay settings,
not something this measurement interprets further.

Prediction check: delay=0 loses (4 markers vanished) AND coalesces (3+ merge
events). delay=0.5 does not lose (0 vanished) and coalesces once. The
predicted DIRECTION and SHAPE both hold. Stated as instructed: if this had
come out the other way, that would need saying plainly too — it did not.
